llowing parameters: (void *parameter, mbedtls_ssl_session *session)\0A *                 If a valid entry is found, it should fill the master of\0A *                 the session object with the cached values and return 0,\0A *                 return 1 otherwise. Optionally peer_cert can be set as well\0A *                 if it is properly present in cache entry.\0A *\0A *                 The set callback is called once during the initial handshake\0A *                 to enable session resuming after the entire handshake has\0A *                 been finished. The set function has the following parameters:\0A *                 (void *parameter, const mbedtls_ssl_session *session). The function\0A *                 should create a cache entry for future retrieval based on\0A *                 the data in the session structure and should keep in mind\0A *                 that the mbedtls_ssl_session object presented (and all its referenced\0A *                 data) is cleared by the SSL/TLS layer when the connection is\0A *                 terminated. It is recommended to add metadata to determine if\0A *                 an entry is still valid in the future. Return 0 if\0A *                 successfully cached, return 1 otherwise.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf           SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam p_cache        parameter (context) for both callbacks\0A * \5Cparam f_get_cache    session get callback\0A * \5Cparam f_set_cache    session set callback\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_session_cache(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                    void *p_cache,\0A                                    mbedtls_ssl_cache_get_t *f_get_cache,\0A                                    mbedtls_ssl_cache_set_t *f_set_cache);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_CLI_C)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Load a session for session resumption.\0A *\0A *                 Sessions loaded through this call will be considered\0A *                 for session resumption in the next handshake.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Even if this call succeeds, it is not guaranteed that\0A *                 the next handshake will indeed be shortened through the\0A *                 use of session resumption: The server is always free\0A *                 to reject any attempt for resumption and fall back to\0A *                 a full handshake.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This function can handle a variety of mechanisms for session\0A *                 resumption: For TLS 1.2, both session ID-based resumption\0A *                 and ticket-based resumption will be considered. For TLS 1.3,\0A *                 sessions equate to tickets, and loading one session by\0A *                 calling this function will lead to its corresponding ticket\0A *                 being advertised as resumption PSK by the client. This\0A *                 depends on session tickets being enabled (see\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_SSL_SESSION_TICKETS configuration option) though.\0A *                 If session tickets are disabled, a call to this function\0A *                 with a TLS 1.3 session, will not have any effect on the next\0A *                 handshake for the SSL context \5Cp ssl.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      The SSL context representing the connection which should\0A *                 be attempted to be setup using session resumption. This\0A *                 must be initialized via mbedtls_ssl_init() and bound to\0A *                 an SSL configuration via mbedtls_ssl_setup(), but\0A *                 the handshake must not yet have been started.\0A * \5Cparam session  The session to be considered for session resumption.\0A *                 This must be a session previously exported via\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_get_session(), and potentially serialized and\0A *                 deserialized through mbedtls_ssl_session_save() and\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_session_load() in the meantime.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc 0 if successful.\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_FEATURE_UNAVAILABLE if the session\0A *                 could not be loaded because one session has already been\0A *                 loaded. This error is non-fatal, and has no observable\0A *                 effect on the SSL context or the session that was attempted\0A *                 to be loaded.\0A * \5Creturn         Another negative error code on other kinds of failure.\0A *\0A * \5Csa             mbedtls_ssl_get_session()\0A * \5Csa             mbedtls_ssl_session_load()\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_set_session(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl, const mbedtls_ssl_session *session);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_CLI_C */\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Load serialized session data into a session structure.\0A *                 On client, this can be used for loading saved sessions\0A *                 before resuming them with mbedtls_ssl_set_session().\0A *                 On server, this can be used for alternative implementations\0A *                 of session cache or session tickets.\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        If a peer certificate chain is associated with the session,\0A *                 the serialized state will only contain the peer's\0A *                 end-entity certificate and the result of the chain\0A *                 verification (unless verification was disabled), but not\0A *                 the rest of the chain.\0A *\0A * \5Csee            mbedtls_ssl_session_save()\0A * \5Csee            mbedtls_ssl_set_session()\0A *\0A * \5Cparam session  The session structure to be populated. It must have been\0A *                 initialised with mbedtls_ssl_session_init() but not\0A *                 populated yet.\0A * \5Cparam buf      The buffer holding the serialized session data. It must be a\0A *                 readable buffer of at least \5Cp len bytes.\0A * \5Cparam len      The size of the serialized data in bytes.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc 0 if successful.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_ALLOC_FAILED if memory allocation failed.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_BAD_INPUT_DATA if input data is invalid.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_VERSION_MISMATCH if the serialized data\0A *                 was generated in a different version or configuration of\0A *                 Mbed TLS.\0A * \5Creturn         Another negative value for other kinds of errors (for\0A *                 example, unsupported features in the embedded certificate).\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_session_load(mbedtls_ssl_session *session,\0A                             const unsigned char *buf,\0A                             size_t len);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Save session structure as serialized data in a buffer.\0A *                 On client, this can be used for saving session data,\0A *                 potentially in non-volatile storage, for resuming later.\0A *                 On server, this can be used for alternative implementations\0A *                 of session cache or session tickets.\0A *\0A * \5Csee            mbedtls_ssl_session_load()\0A *\0A * \5Cparam session  The session structure to be saved.\0A * \5Cparam buf      The buffer to write the serialized data to. It must be a\0A *                 writeable buffer of at least \5Cp buf_len bytes, or may be \5Cc\0A *                 NULL if \5Cp buf_len is \5Cc 0.\0A * \5Cparam buf_len  The number of bytes available for writing in \5Cp buf.\0A * \5Cparam olen     The size in bytes of the data that has been or would have\0A *                 been written. It must point to a valid \5Cc size_t.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           \5Cp olen is updated to the correct value regardless of\0A *                 whether \5Cp buf_len was large enough. This makes it possible\0A *                 to determine the necessary size by calling this function\0A *                 with \5Cp buf set to \5Cc NULL and \5Cp buf_len to \5Cc 0.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           For TLS 1.3 sessions, this feature is supported only if the\0A *                 MBEDTLS_SSL_SESSION_TICKETS configuration option is enabled,\0A *                 as in TLS 1.3 session resumption is possible only with\0A *                 tickets.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc 0 if successful.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL if \5Cp buf is too small.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_FEATURE_UNAVAILABLE if the\0A *                 MBEDTLS_SSL_SESSION_TICKETS configuration option is disabled\0A *                 and the session is a TLS 1.3 session.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_session_save(const mbedtls_ssl_session *session,\0A                             unsigned char *buf,\0A                             size_t buf_len,\0A                             size_t *olen);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief               Set the list of allowed ciphersuites and the preference\0A *                      order. First in the list has the highest preference.\0A *\0A *                      For TLS 1.2, the notion of ciphersuite determines both\0A *                      the key exchange mechanism and the suite of symmetric\0A *                      algorithms to be used during and after the handshake.\0A *\0A *                      For TLS 1.3 (in development), the notion of ciphersuite\0A *                      only determines the suite of symmetric algorithms to be\0A *                      used during and after the handshake, while key exchange\0A *                      mechanisms are configured separately.\0A *\0A *                      In Mbed TLS, ciphersuites for both TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3\0A *                      are configured via this function. For users of TLS 1.3,\0A *                      there will be separate API for the configuration of key\0A *                      exchange mechanisms.\0A *\0A *                      The list of ciphersuites passed to this function may\0A *                      contain a mixture of TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 ciphersuite\0A *                      identifiers. This is useful if negotiation of TLS 1.3\0A *                      should be attempted, but a fallback to TLS 1.2 would\0A *                      be tolerated.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote                By default, the server chooses its preferred\0A *                      ciphersuite among those that the client supports. If\0A *                      mbedtls_ssl_conf_preference_order() is called to prefer\0A *                      the client's preferences, the server instead chooses\0A *                      the client's preferred ciphersuite among those that\0A *                      the server supports.\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning             The ciphersuites array \5Cp ciphersuites is not copied.\0A *                      It must remain valid for the lifetime of the SSL\0A *                      configuration \5Cp conf.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf          The SSL configuration to modify.\0A * \5Cparam ciphersuites  A 0-terminated list of IANA identifiers of supported\0A *                      ciphersuites, accessible through \5Cc MBEDTLS_TLS_XXX\0A *                      and \5Cc MBEDTLS_TLS1_3_XXX macros defined in\0A *                      ssl_ciphersuites.h.\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_ciphersuites(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                   const int *ciphersuites);\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_PROTO_TLS1_3)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief Set the supported key exchange modes for TLS 1.3 connections.\0A *\0A *        In contrast to TLS 1.2, the ciphersuite concept in TLS 1.3 does not\0A *        include the choice of key exchange mechanism. It is therefore not\0A *        covered by the API mbedtls_ssl_conf_ciphersuites(). See the\0A *        documentation of mbedtls_ssl_conf_ciphersuites() for more\0A *        information on the ciphersuite concept in TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3.\0A *\0A *        The present function is specific to TLS 1.3 and allows users to\0A *        configure the set of supported key exchange mechanisms in TLS 1.3.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf       The SSL configuration the change should apply to.\0A * \5Cparam kex_modes  A bitwise combination of one or more of the following:\0A *                   - MBEDTLS_SSL_TLS1_3_KEY_EXCHANGE_MODE_PSK\0A *                     This flag enables pure-PSK key exchanges.\0A *                   - MBEDTLS_SSL_TLS1_3_KEY_EXCHANGE_MODE_PSK_EPHEMERAL\0A *                     This flag enables combined PSK-ephemeral key exchanges.\0A *                   - MBEDTLS_SSL_TLS1_3_KEY_EXCHANGE_MODE_EPHEMERAL\0A *                     This flag enables pure-ephemeral key exchanges.\0A *                   For convenience, the following pre-defined macros are\0A *                   available for combinations of the above:\0A *                   - MBEDTLS_SSL_TLS1_3_KEY_EXCHANGE_MODE_ALL\0A *                     Includes all of pure-PSK, PSK-ephemeral and pure-ephemeral.\0A *                   - MBEDTLS_SSL_TLS1_3_KEY_EXCHANGE_MODE_PSK_ALL\0A *                     Includes both pure-PSK and combined PSK-ephemeral\0A *                     key exchanges, but excludes pure-ephemeral key exchanges.\0A *                   - MBEDTLS_SSL_TLS1_3_KEY_EXCHANGE_MODE_EPHEMERAL_ALL\0A *                     Includes both pure-ephemeral and combined PSK-ephemeral\0A *                     key exchanges.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote  If a PSK-based key exchange mode shall be supported, applications\0A *        must also use the APIs mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk() or\0A *        mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk_cb() or mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk_opaque()\0A *        to configure the PSKs to be used.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote  If a pure-ephemeral key exchange mode shall be supported,\0A *        server-side applications must also provide a certificate via\0A *        mbedtls_ssl_conf_own_cert().\0A *\0A */\0A\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_tls13_key_exchange_modes(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                               const int kex_modes);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_PROTO_TLS1_3 */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_DTLS_CONNECTION_ID)\0A#define MBEDTLS_SSL_UNEXPECTED_CID_IGNORE 0\0A#define MBEDTLS_SSL_UNEXPECTED_CID_FAIL   1\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief               Specify the length of Connection IDs for incoming\0A *                      encrypted DTLS records, as well as the behaviour\0A *                      on unexpected CIDs.\0A *\0A *                      By default, the CID length is set to \5Cc 0,\0A *                      and unexpected CIDs are silently ignored.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf          The SSL configuration to modify.\0A * \5Cparam len           The length in Bytes of the CID fields in encrypted\0A *                      DTLS records using the CID mechanism. This must\0A *                      not be larger than #MBEDTLS_SSL_CID_OUT_LEN_MAX.\0A * \5Cparam ignore_other_cids This determines the stack's behaviour when\0A *                          receiving a record with an unexpected CID.\0A *                          Possible values are:\0A *                          - #MBEDTLS_SSL_UNEXPECTED_CID_IGNORE\0A *                            In this case, the record is silently ignored.\0A *                          - #MBEDTLS_SSL_UNEXPECTED_CID_FAIL\0A *                            In this case, the stack fails with the specific\0A *                            error code #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_UNEXPECTED_CID.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote                The CID specification allows implementations to either\0A *                      use a common length for all incoming connection IDs or\0A *                      allow variable-length incoming IDs. Mbed TLS currently\0A *                      requires a common length for all connections sharing the\0A *                      same SSL configuration; this allows simpler parsing of\0A *                      record headers.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn              \5Cc 0 on success.\0A * \5Creturn              #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_BAD_INPUT_DATA if \5Cp len\0A *                      is too large.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_conf_cid(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf, size_t len,\0A                         int ignore_other_cids);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_DTLS_CONNECTION_ID */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_X509_CRT_PARSE_C)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the X.509 security profile used for verification\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The restrictions are enforced for all certificates in the\0A *                 chain. However, signatures in the handshake are not covered\0A *                 by this setting but by \5Cb mbedtls_ssl_conf_sig_hashes().\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam profile  Profile to use\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_cert_profile(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                   const mbedtls_x509_crt_profile *profile);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the data required to verify peer certificate\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           See \5Cc mbedtls_x509_crt_verify() for notes regarding the\0A *                 parameters ca_chain (maps to trust_ca for that function)\0A *                 and ca_crl.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam ca_chain trusted CA chain (meaning all fully trusted top-level CAs)\0A * \5Cparam ca_crl   trusted CA CRLs\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_ca_chain(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                               mbedtls_x509_crt *ca_chain,\0A                               mbedtls_x509_crl *ca_crl);\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_KEY_EXCHANGE_CERT_REQ_ALLOWED_ENABLED)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set DN hints sent to client in CertificateRequest message\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           If not set, subject distinguished names (DNs) are taken\0A *                 from \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_ca_chain()\0A *                 or \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_ca_chain())\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam crt      crt chain whose subject DNs are issuer DNs of client certs\0A *                 from which the client should select client peer certificate.\0A */\0Astatic inline\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_dn_hints(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                               const mbedtls_x509_crt *crt)\0A{\0A    conf->MBEDTLS_PRIVATE(dn_hints) = crt;\0A}\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_KEY_EXCHANGE_CERT_REQ_ALLOWED_ENABLED */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_X509_TRUSTED_CERTIFICATE_CALLBACK)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the trusted certificate callback.\0A *\0A *                 This API allows to register the set of trusted certificates\0A *                 through a callback, instead of a linked list as configured\0A *                 by mbedtls_ssl_conf_ca_chain().\0A *\0A *                 This is useful for example in contexts where a large number\0A *                 of CAs are used, and the inefficiency of maintaining them\0A *                 in a linked list cannot be tolerated. It is also useful when\0A *                 the set of trusted CAs needs to be modified frequently.\0A *\0A *                 See the documentation of `mbedtls_x509_crt_ca_cb_t` for\0A *                 more information.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     The SSL configuration to register the callback with.\0A * \5Cparam f_ca_cb  The trusted certificate callback to use when verifying\0A *                 certificate chains.\0A * \5Cparam p_ca_cb  The context to be passed to \5Cp f_ca_cb (for example,\0A *                 a reference to a trusted CA database).\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This API is incompatible with mbedtls_ssl_conf_ca_chain():\0A *                 Any call to this function overwrites the values set through\0A *                 earlier calls to mbedtls_ssl_conf_ca_chain() or\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_conf_ca_cb().\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This API is incompatible with CA indication in\0A *                 CertificateRequest messages: A server-side SSL context which\0A *                 is bound to an SSL configuration that uses a CA callback\0A *                 configured via mbedtls_ssl_conf_ca_cb(), and which requires\0A *                 client authentication, will send an empty CA list in the\0A *                 corresponding CertificateRequest message.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This API is incompatible with mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_ca_chain():\0A *                 If an SSL context is bound to an SSL configuration which uses\0A *                 CA callbacks configured via mbedtls_ssl_conf_ca_cb(), then\0A *                 calls to mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_ca_chain() have no effect.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The use of this API disables the use of restartable ECC\0A *                 during X.509 CRT signature verification (but doesn't affect\0A *                 other uses).\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        This API is incompatible with the use of CRLs. Any call to\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_conf_ca_cb() unsets CRLs configured through\0A *                 earlier calls to mbedtls_ssl_conf_ca_chain().\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        In multi-threaded environments, the callback \5Cp f_ca_cb\0A *                 must be thread-safe, and it is the user's responsibility\0A *                 to guarantee this (for example through a mutex\0A *                 contained in the callback context pointed to by \5Cp p_ca_cb).\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_ca_cb(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                            mbedtls_x509_crt_ca_cb_t f_ca_cb,\0A                            void *p_ca_cb);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_X509_TRUSTED_CERTIFICATE_CALLBACK */\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set own certificate chain and private key\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           own_cert should contain in order from the bottom up your\0A *                 certificate chain. The top certificate (self-signed)\0A *                 can be omitted.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           On server, this function can be called multiple times to\0A *                 provision more than one cert/key pair (eg one ECDSA, one\0A *                 RSA with SHA-256, one RSA with SHA-1). An adequate\0A *                 certificate will be selected according to the client's\0A *                 advertised capabilities. In case multiple certificates are\0A *                 adequate, preference is given to the one set by the first\0A *                 call to this function, then second, etc.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           On client, only the first call has any effect. That is,\0A *                 only one client certificate can be provisioned. The\0A *                 server's preferences in its CertificateRequest message will\0A *                 be ignored and our only cert will be sent regardless of\0A *                 whether it matches those preferences - the server can then\0A *                 decide what it wants to do with it.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The provided \5Cp pk_key needs to match the public key in the\0A *                 first certificate in \5Cp own_cert, or all handshakes using\0A *                 that certificate will fail. It is your responsibility\0A *                 to ensure that; this function will not perform any check.\0A *                 You may use mbedtls_pk_check_pair() in order to perform\0A *                 this check yourself, but be aware that this function can\0A *                 be computationally expensive on some key types.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam own_cert own public certificate chain\0A * \5Cparam pk_key   own private key\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         0 on success or MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_ALLOC_FAILED\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_conf_own_cert(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                              mbedtls_x509_crt *own_cert,\0A                              mbedtls_pk_context *pk_key);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_X509_CRT_PARSE_C */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_HANDSHAKE_WITH_PSK_ENABLED)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Configure pre-shared keys (PSKs) and their\0A *                 identities to be used in PSK-based ciphersuites.\0A *\0A *                 Only one PSK can be registered, through either\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk() or mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk_opaque().\0A *                 If you attempt to register more than one PSK, this function\0A *                 fails, though this may change in future versions, which\0A *                 may add support for multiple PSKs.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This is mainly useful for clients. Servers will usually\0A *                 want to use \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk_cb() instead.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           A PSK set by \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_psk() in the PSK callback\0A *                 takes precedence over a PSK configured by this function.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     The SSL configuration to register the PSK with.\0A * \5Cparam psk      The pointer to the pre-shared key to use.\0A * \5Cparam psk_len  The length of the pre-shared key in bytes.\0A * \5Cparam psk_identity      The pointer to the pre-shared key identity.\0A * \5Cparam psk_identity_len  The length of the pre-shared key identity\0A *                          in bytes.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The PSK and its identity are copied internally and\0A *                 hence need not be preserved by the caller for the lifetime\0A *                 of the SSL configuration.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc 0 if successful.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_FEATURE_UNAVAILABLE if no more PSKs\0A *                 can be configured. In this case, the old PSK(s) remain intact.\0A * \5Creturn         Another negative error code on other kinds of failure.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                         const unsigned char *psk, size_t psk_len,\0A                         const unsigned char *psk_identity, size_t psk_identity_len);\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_USE_PSA_CRYPTO)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Configure one or more opaque pre-shared keys (PSKs) and\0A *                 their identities to be used in PSK-based ciphersuites.\0A *\0A *                 Only one PSK can be registered, through either\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk() or mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk_opaque().\0A *                 If you attempt to register more than one PSK, this function\0A *                 fails, though this may change in future versions, which\0A *                 may add support for multiple PSKs.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This is mainly useful for clients. Servers will usually\0A *                 want to use \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk_cb() instead.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           An opaque PSK set by \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_psk_opaque() in\0A *                 the PSK callback takes precedence over an opaque PSK\0A *                 configured by this function.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     The SSL configuration to register the PSK with.\0A * \5Cparam psk      The identifier of the key slot holding the PSK.\0A *                 Until \5Cp conf is destroyed or this function is successfully\0A *                 called again, the key slot \5Cp psk must be populated with a\0A *                 key of type PSA_ALG_CATEGORY_KEY_DERIVATION whose policy\0A *                 allows its use for the key derivation algorithm applied\0A *                 in the handshake.\0A * \5Cparam psk_identity      The pointer to the pre-shared key identity.\0A * \5Cparam psk_identity_len  The length of the pre-shared key identity\0A *                          in bytes.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The PSK identity hint is copied internally and hence need\0A *                 not be preserved by the caller for the lifetime of the\0A *                 SSL configuration.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc 0 if successful.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_FEATURE_UNAVAILABLE if no more PSKs\0A *                 can be configured. In this case, the old PSK(s) remain intact.\0A * \5Creturn         Another negative error code on other kinds of failure.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk_opaque(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                mbedtls_svc_key_id_t psk,\0A                                const unsigned char *psk_identity,\0A                                size_t psk_identity_len);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_USE_PSA_CRYPTO */\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the pre-shared Key (PSK) for the current handshake.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This should only be called inside the PSK callback,\0A *                 i.e. the function passed to \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk_cb().\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           A PSK set by this function takes precedence over a PSK\0A *                 configured by \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk().\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      The SSL context to configure a PSK for.\0A * \5Cparam psk      The pointer to the pre-shared key.\0A * \5Cparam psk_len  The length of the pre-shared key in bytes.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc 0 if successful.\0A * \5Creturn         An \5Cc MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_XXX error code on failure.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_psk(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                           const unsigned char *psk, size_t psk_len);\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_USE_PSA_CRYPTO)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set an opaque pre-shared Key (PSK) for the current handshake.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This should only be called inside the PSK callback,\0A *                 i.e. the function passed to \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk_cb().\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           An opaque PSK set by this function takes precedence over an\0A *                 opaque PSK configured by \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk_opaque().\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      The SSL context to configure a PSK for.\0A * \5Cparam psk      The identifier of the key slot holding the PSK.\0A *                 For the duration of the current handshake, the key slot\0A *                 must be populated with a key of type\0A *                 PSA_ALG_CATEGORY_KEY_DERIVATION whose policy allows its\0A *                 use for the key derivation algorithm\0A *                 applied in the handshake.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc 0 if successful.\0A * \5Creturn         An \5Cc MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_XXX error code on failure.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_psk_opaque(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                                  mbedtls_svc_key_id_t psk);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_USE_PSA_CRYPTO */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the PSK callback (server-side only).\0A *\0A *                 If set, the PSK callback is called for each\0A *                 handshake where a PSK-based ciphersuite was negotiated.\0A *                 The caller provides the identity received and wants to\0A *                 receive the actual PSK data and length.\0A *\0A *                 The callback has the following parameters:\0A *                 - \5Cc void*: The opaque pointer \5Cp p_psk.\0A *                 - \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_context*: The SSL context to which\0A *                                            the operation applies.\0A *                 - \5Cc const unsigned char*: The PSK identity\0A *                                            selected by the client.\0A *                 - \5Cc size_t: The length of the PSK identity\0A *                              selected by the client.\0A *\0A *                 If a valid PSK identity is found, the callback should use\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_psk() or\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_psk_opaque()\0A *                 on the SSL context to set the correct PSK and return \5Cc 0.\0A *                 Any other return value will result in a denied PSK identity.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           A dynamic PSK (i.e. set by the PSK callback) takes\0A *                 precedence over a static PSK (i.e. set by\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk() or\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk_opaque()).\0A *                 This means that if you set a PSK callback using this\0A *                 function, you don't need to set a PSK using\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk() or\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk_opaque()).\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     The SSL configuration to register the callback with.\0A * \5Cparam f_psk    The callback for selecting and setting the PSK based\0A *                 in the PSK identity chosen by the client.\0A * \5Cparam p_psk    A pointer to an opaque structure to be passed to\0A *                 the callback, for example a PSK store.\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_psk_cb(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                             int (*f_psk)(void *, mbedtls_ssl_context *, const unsigned char *,\0A                                          size_t),\0A                             void *p_psk);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C */\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_HANDSHAKE_WITH_PSK_ENABLED */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_DHM_C) && defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the Diffie-Hellman public P and G values\0A *                 from big-endian binary presentations.\0A *                 (Default values: MBEDTLS_DHM_RFC3526_MODP_2048_[PG]_BIN)\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam dhm_P    Diffie-Hellman-Merkle modulus in big-endian binary form\0A * \5Cparam P_len    Length of DHM modulus\0A * \5Cparam dhm_G    Diffie-Hellman-Merkle generator in big-endian binary form\0A * \5Cparam G_len    Length of DHM generator\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         0 if successful\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_conf_dh_param_bin(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                  const unsigned char *dhm_P, size_t P_len,\0A                                  const unsigned char *dhm_G,  size_t G_len);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the Diffie-Hellman public P and G values,\0A *                 read from existing context (server-side only)\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam dhm_ctx  Diffie-Hellman-Merkle context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         0 if successful\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_conf_dh_param_ctx(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf, mbedtls_dhm_context *dhm_ctx);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_DHM_C && defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C) */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_DHM_C) && defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_CLI_C)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the minimum length for Diffie-Hellman parameters.\0A *                 (Client-side only.)\0A *                 (Default: 1024 bits.)\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam bitlen   Minimum bit length of the DHM prime\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_dhm_min_bitlen(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                     unsigned int bitlen);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_DHM_C && MBEDTLS_SSL_CLI_C */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_ECP_C)\0A#if !defined(MBEDTLS_DEPRECATED_REMOVED)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the allowed curves in order of preference.\0A *\0A *                 On server: this only affects selection of the ECDHE curve;\0A *                 the curves used for ECDH and ECDSA are determined by the\0A *                 list of available certificates instead.\0A *\0A *                 On client: this affects the list of curves offered for any\0A *                 use. The server can override our preference order.\0A *\0A *                 Both sides: limits the set of curves accepted for use in\0A *                 ECDHE and in the peer's end-entity certificate.\0A *\0A * \5Cdeprecated     Superseded by mbedtls_ssl_conf_groups().\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This has no influence on which curves are allowed inside the\0A *                 certificate chains, see \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_cert_profile()\0A *                 for that. For the end-entity certificate however, the key\0A *                 will be accepted only if it is allowed both by this list\0A *                 and by the cert profile.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This list should be ordered by decreasing preference\0A *                 (preferred curve first).\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The default list is the same set of curves that\0A *                 #mbedtls_x509_crt_profile_default allows, plus\0A *                 ECDHE-only curves selected according to the same criteria.\0A *                 The order favors curves with the lowest resource usage.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           New minor versions of Mbed TLS may extend this list,\0A *                 for example if new curves are added to the library.\0A *                 New minor versions of Mbed TLS will not remove items\0A *                 from this list unless serious security concerns require it.\0A *                 New minor versions of Mbed TLS may change the order in\0A *                 keeping with the general principle of favoring the lowest\0A *                 resource usage.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam curves   Ordered list of allowed curves,\0A *                 terminated by MBEDTLS_ECP_DP_NONE.\0A */\0Avoid MBEDTLS_DEPRECATED mbedtls_ssl_conf_curves(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                                const mbedtls_ecp_group_id *curves);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_DEPRECATED_REMOVED */\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_ECP_C */\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the allowed groups in order of preference.\0A *\0A *                 On server: This only affects the choice of key agreement mechanism\0A *\0A *                 On client: this affects the list of groups offered for any\0A *                 use. The server can override our preference order.\0A *\0A *                 Both sides: limits the set of groups accepted for use in\0A *                 key sharing.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This function replaces the deprecated mbedtls_ssl_conf_curves(),\0A *                 which only allows ECP curves to be configured.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The most recent invocation of either mbedtls_ssl_conf_curves()\0A *                 or mbedtls_ssl_conf_groups() nullifies all previous invocations\0A *                 of both.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This list should be ordered by decreasing preference\0A *                 (preferred group first).\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           When this function is not called, a default list is used,\0A *                 consisting of all supported curves at 255 bits and above,\0A *                 and all supported finite fields at 2048 bits and above.\0A *                 The order favors groups with the lowest resource usage.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           New minor versions of Mbed TLS will not remove items\0A *                 from the default list unless serious security concerns require it.\0A *                 New minor versions of Mbed TLS may change the order in\0A *                 keeping with the general principle of favoring the lowest\0A *                 resource usage.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam groups   List of allowed groups ordered by preference, terminated by 0.\0A *                 Must contain valid IANA NamedGroup IDs (provided via either an integer\0A *                 or using MBEDTLS_TLS1_3_NAMED_GROUP_XXX macros).\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_groups(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                             const uint16_t *groups);\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_HANDSHAKE_WITH_CERT_ENABLED)\0A#if !defined(MBEDTLS_DEPRECATED_REMOVED) && defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_PROTO_TLS1_2)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the allowed hashes for signatures during the handshake.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This only affects which hashes are offered and can be used\0A *                 for signatures during the handshake. Hashes for message\0A *                 authentication and the TLS PRF are controlled by the\0A *                 ciphersuite, see \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_ciphersuites(). Hashes\0A *                 used for certificate signature are controlled by the\0A *                 verification profile, see \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_cert_profile().\0A *\0A * \5Cdeprecated     Superseded by mbedtls_ssl_conf_sig_algs().\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This list should be ordered by decreasing preference\0A *                 (preferred hash first).\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           By default, all supported hashes whose length is at least\0A *                 256 bits are allowed. This is the same set as the default\0A *                 for certificate verification\0A *                 (#mbedtls_x509_crt_profile_default).\0A *                 The preference order is currently unspecified and may\0A *                 change in future versions.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           New minor versions of Mbed TLS may extend this list,\0A *                 for example if new curves are added to the library.\0A *                 New minor versions of Mbed TLS will not remove items\0A *                 from this list unless serious security concerns require it.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam hashes   Ordered list of allowed signature hashes,\0A *                 terminated by \5Cc MBEDTLS_MD_NONE.\0A */\0Avoid MBEDTLS_DEPRECATED mbedtls_ssl_conf_sig_hashes(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                                    const int *hashes);\0A#endif /* !MBEDTLS_DEPRECATED_REMOVED && MBEDTLS_SSL_PROTO_TLS1_2 */\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Configure allowed signature algorithms for use in TLS\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     The SSL configuration to use.\0A * \5Cparam sig_algs List of allowed IANA values for TLS 1.3 signature algorithms,\0A *                 terminated by #MBEDTLS_TLS1_3_SIG_NONE. The list must remain\0A *                 available throughout the lifetime of the conf object.\0A *                 - For TLS 1.3, values of \5Cc MBEDTLS_TLS1_3_SIG_XXXX should be\0A *                   used.\0A *                 - For TLS 1.2, values should be given as\0A *                   \22(HashAlgorithm << 8) | SignatureAlgorithm\22.\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_sig_algs(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                               const uint16_t *sig_algs);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_HANDSHAKE_WITH_CERT_ENABLED */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_X509_CRT_PARSE_C)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set or reset the hostname to check against the received\0A *                 peer certificate. On a client, this also sets the\0A *                 ServerName TLS extension, if that extension is enabled.\0A *                 On a TLS 1.3 client, this also sets the server name in\0A *                 the session resumption ticket, if that feature is enabled.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A * \5Cparam hostname The server hostname. This may be \5Cc NULL to clear\0A *                 the hostname.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Maximum hostname length #MBEDTLS_SSL_MAX_HOST_NAME_LEN.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           If the hostname is \5Cc NULL on a client, then the server\0A *                 is not authenticated: it only needs to have a valid\0A *                 certificate, not a certificate matching its name.\0A *                 Therefore you should always call this function on a client,\0A *                 unless the connection is set up to only allow\0A *                 pre-shared keys, or in scenarios where server\0A *                 impersonation is not a concern. See the documentation of\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CERTIFICATE_VERIFICATION_WITHOUT_HOSTNAME\0A *                 for more details.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         0 if successful, #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_ALLOC_FAILED on\0A *                 allocation failure, #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_BAD_INPUT_DATA on\0A *                 too long input hostname.\0A *\0A *                 Hostname set to the one provided on success (cleared\0A *                 when NULL). On allocation failure hostname is cleared.\0A *                 On too long input failure, old hostname is unchanged.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_set_hostname(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl, const char *hostname);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Get the hostname that checked against the received\0A *                 server certificate. It is used to set the ServerName\0A *                 TLS extension, too, if that extension is enabled.\0A *                 (client-side only)\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         const pointer to the hostname value\0A */\0Astatic inline const char *mbedtls_ssl_get_hostname(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl)\0A{\0A    return ssl->MBEDTLS_PRIVATE(hostname);\0A}\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_X509_CRT_PARSE_C */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_SERVER_NAME_INDICATION)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Retrieve SNI extension value for the current handshake.\0A *                 Available in \5Cc f_cert_cb of \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_cert_cb(),\0A *                 this is the same value passed to \5Cc f_sni callback of\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_sni() and may be used instead of\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_sni().\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A * \5Cparam name_len pointer into which to store length of returned value.\0A *                 0 if SNI extension is not present or not yet processed.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         const pointer to SNI extension value.\0A *                 - value is valid only when called in \5Cc f_cert_cb\0A *                   registered with \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_cert_cb().\0A *                 - value is NULL if SNI extension is not present.\0A *                 - value is not '\5C0'-terminated. Use \5Cc name_len for len.\0A *                 - value must not be freed.\0A */\0Aconst unsigned char *mbedtls_ssl_get_hs_sni(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                                            size_t *name_len);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set own certificate and key for the current handshake\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Same as \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_own_cert() but for use within\0A *                 the SNI callback or the certificate selection callback.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Passing null \5Cc own_cert clears the certificate list for\0A *                 the current handshake.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A * \5Cparam own_cert own public certificate chain\0A * \5Cparam pk_key   own private key\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         0 on success or MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_ALLOC_FAILED\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_own_cert(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                                mbedtls_x509_crt *own_cert,\0A                                mbedtls_pk_context *pk_key);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the data required to verify peer certificate for the\0A *                 current handshake\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Same as \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_ca_chain() but for use within\0A *                 the SNI callback or the certificate selection callback.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A * \5Cparam ca_chain trusted CA chain (meaning all fully trusted top-level CAs)\0A * \5Cparam ca_crl   trusted CA CRLs\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_ca_chain(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                                 mbedtls_x509_crt *ca_chain,\0A                                 mbedtls_x509_crl *ca_crl);\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_KEY_EXCHANGE_CERT_REQ_ALLOWED_ENABLED)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set DN hints sent to client in CertificateRequest message\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Same as \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_dn_hints() but for use within\0A *                 the SNI callback or the certificate selection callback.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A * \5Cparam crt      crt chain whose subject DNs are issuer DNs of client certs\0A *                 from which the client should select client peer certificate.\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_dn_hints(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                                 const mbedtls_x509_crt *crt);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_KEY_EXCHANGE_CERT_REQ_ALLOWED_ENABLED */\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set authmode for the current handshake.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Same as \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_authmode() but for use within\0A *                 the SNI callback or the certificate selection callback.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A * \5Cparam authmode MBEDTLS_SSL_VERIFY_NONE, MBEDTLS_SSL_VERIFY_OPTIONAL or\0A *                 MBEDTLS_SSL_VERIFY_REQUIRED\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_authmode(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                                 int authmode);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set server side ServerName TLS extension callback\0A *                 (optional, server-side only).\0A *\0A *                 If set, the ServerName callback is called whenever the\0A *                 server receives a ServerName TLS extension from the client\0A *                 during a handshake. The ServerName callback has the\0A *                 following parameters: (void *parameter, mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A *                 const unsigned char *hostname, size_t len). If a suitable\0A *                 certificate is found, the callback must set the\0A *                 certificate(s) and key(s) to use with \5Cc\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_own_cert() (can be called repeatedly),\0A *                 and may optionally adjust the CA and associated CRL with \5Cc\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_ca_chain() as well as the client\0A *                 authentication mode with \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_authmode(),\0A *                 then must return 0. If no matching name is found, the\0A *                 callback may return non-zero to abort the handshake.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam f_sni    verification function\0A * \5Cparam p_sni    verification parameter\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_sni(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                          int (*f_sni)(void *, mbedtls_ssl_context *, const unsigned char *,\0A                                       size_t),\0A                          void *p_sni);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_SERVER_NAME_INDICATION */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_KEY_EXCHANGE_ECJPAKE_ENABLED)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the EC J-PAKE password for current handshake.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           An internal copy is made, and destroyed as soon as the\0A *                 handshake is completed, or when the SSL context is reset or\0A *                 freed.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The SSL context needs to be already set up. The right place\0A *                 to call this function is between \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_setup() or\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_reset() and \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_handshake().\0A *                 Password cannot be empty (see RFC 8236).\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A * \5Cparam pw       EC J-PAKE password (pre-shared secret). It cannot be empty\0A * \5Cparam pw_len   length of pw in bytes\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         0 on success, or a negative error code.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_ecjpake_password(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                                        const unsigned char *pw,\0A                                        size_t pw_len);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the EC J-PAKE opaque password for current handshake.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The key must remain valid until the handshake is over.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The SSL context needs to be already set up. The right place\0A *                 to call this function is between \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_setup() or\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_reset() and \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_handshake().\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A * \5Cparam pwd      EC J-PAKE opaque password\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         0 on success, or a negative error code.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_set_hs_ecjpake_password_opaque(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                                               mbedtls_svc_key_id_t pwd);\0A#endif /*MBEDTLS_KEY_EXCHANGE_ECJPAKE_ENABLED */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_ALPN)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the supported Application Layer Protocols.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam protos   Pointer to a NULL-terminated list of supported protocols,\0A *                 in decreasing preference order. The pointer to the list is\0A *                 recorded by the library for later reference as required, so\0A *                 the lifetime of the table must be at least as long as the\0A *                 lifetime of the SSL configuration structure.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         0 on success, or MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_BAD_INPUT_DATA.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_conf_alpn_protocols(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf, const char **protos);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Get the name of the negotiated Application Layer Protocol.\0A *                 This function should be called after the handshake is\0A *                 completed.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         Protocol name, or NULL if no protocol was negotiated.\0A */\0Aconst char *mbedtls_ssl_get_alpn_protocol(const mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_ALPN */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_DTLS_SRTP)\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_DEBUG_C)\0Astatic inline const char *mbedtls_ssl_get_srtp_profile_as_string(mbedtls_ssl_srtp_profile profile)\0A{\0A    switch (profile) {\0A        case MBEDTLS_TLS_SRTP_AES128_CM_HMAC_SHA1_80:\0A            return \22MBEDTLS_TLS_SRTP_AES128_CM_HMAC_SHA1_80\22;\0A        case MBEDTLS_TLS_SRTP_AES128_CM_HMAC_SHA1_32:\0A            return \22MBEDTLS_TLS_SRTP_AES128_CM_HMAC_SHA1_32\22;\0A        case MBEDTLS_TLS_SRTP_NULL_HMAC_SHA1_80:\0A            return \22MBEDTLS_TLS_SRTP_NULL_HMAC_SHA1_80\22;\0A        case MBEDTLS_TLS_SRTP_NULL_HMAC_SHA1_32:\0A            return \22MBEDTLS_TLS_SRTP_NULL_HMAC_SHA1_32\22;\0A        default: break;\0A    }\0A    return \22\22;\0A}\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_DEBUG_C */\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief                   Manage support for mki(master key id) value\0A *                          in use_srtp extension.\0A *                          MKI is an optional part of SRTP used for key management\0A *                          and re-keying. See RFC3711 section 3.1 for details.\0A *                          The default value is\0A *                          #MBEDTLS_SSL_DTLS_SRTP_MKI_UNSUPPORTED.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf              The SSL configuration to manage mki support.\0A * \5Cparam support_mki_value Enable or disable mki usage. Values are\0A *                          #MBEDTLS_SSL_DTLS_SRTP_MKI_UNSUPPORTED\0A *                          or #MBEDTLS_SSL_DTLS_SRTP_MKI_SUPPORTED.\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_srtp_mki_value_supported(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                               int support_mki_value);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief                   Set the supported DTLS-SRTP protection profiles.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf              SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam profiles          Pointer to a List of MBEDTLS_TLS_SRTP_UNSET terminated\0A *                          supported protection profiles\0A *                          in decreasing preference order.\0A *                          The pointer to the list is recorded by the library\0A *                          for later reference as required, so the lifetime\0A *                          of the table must be at least as long as the lifetime\0A *                          of the SSL configuration structure.\0A *                          The list must not hold more than\0A *                          MBEDTLS_TLS_SRTP_MAX_PROFILE_LIST_LENGTH elements\0A *                          (excluding the terminating MBEDTLS_TLS_SRTP_UNSET).\0A *\0A * \5Creturn                  0 on success\0A * \5Creturn                  #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_BAD_INPUT_DATA when the list of\0A *                          protection profiles is incorrect.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_conf_dtls_srtp_protection_profiles\0A    (mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A    const mbedtls_ssl_srtp_profile *profiles);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief                  Set the mki_value for the current DTLS-SRTP session.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl              SSL context to use.\0A * \5Cparam mki_value        The MKI value to set.\0A * \5Cparam mki_len          The length of the MKI value.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote                   This function is relevant on client side only.\0A *                         The server discovers the mki value during handshake.\0A *                         A mki value set on server side using this function\0A *                         is ignored.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn                 0 on success\0A * \5Creturn                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_BAD_INPUT_DATA\0A * \5Creturn                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_FEATURE_UNAVAILABLE\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_dtls_srtp_set_mki_value(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                                        unsigned char *mki_value,\0A                                        uint16_t mki_len);\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief                  Get the negotiated DTLS-SRTP information:\0A *                         Protection profile and MKI value.\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning                This function must be called after the handshake is\0A *                         completed. The value returned by this function must\0A *                         not be trusted or acted upon before the handshake completes.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl              The SSL context to query.\0A * \5Cparam dtls_srtp_info   The negotiated DTLS-SRTP information:\0A *                         - Protection profile in use.\0A *                         A direct mapping of the iana defined value for protection\0A *                         profile on an uint16_t.\0A                   http://www.iana.org/assignments/srtp-protection/srtp-protection.xhtml\0A *                         #MBEDTLS_TLS_SRTP_UNSET if the use of SRTP was not negotiated\0A *                         or peer's Hello packet was not parsed yet.\0A *                         - mki size and value( if size is > 0 ).\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_get_dtls_srtp_negotiation_result(const mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                                                  mbedtls_dtls_srtp_info *dtls_srtp_info);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_DTLS_SRTP */\0A\0A#if !defined(MBEDTLS_DEPRECATED_REMOVED)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the maximum supported version sent from the client side\0A *                 and/or accepted at the server side.\0A *\0A *                 See also the documentation of mbedtls_ssl_conf_min_version().\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This ignores ciphersuites from higher versions.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This function is deprecated and has been replaced by\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_max_tls_version().\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam major    Major version number (#MBEDTLS_SSL_MAJOR_VERSION_3)\0A * \5Cparam minor    Minor version number\0A *                 (#MBEDTLS_SSL_MINOR_VERSION_3 for (D)TLS 1.2,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_SSL_MINOR_VERSION_4 for TLS 1.3)\0A */\0Avoid MBEDTLS_DEPRECATED mbedtls_ssl_conf_max_version(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf, int major,\0A                                                     int minor);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_DEPRECATED_REMOVED */\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the maximum supported version sent from the client side\0A *                 and/or accepted at the server side.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           After the handshake, you can call\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_get_version_number() to see what version was\0A *                 negotiated.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf         SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam tls_version  TLS protocol version number (\5Cc mbedtls_ssl_protocol_version)\0A *                     (#MBEDTLS_SSL_VERSION_UNKNOWN is not valid)\0A */\0Astatic inline void mbedtls_ssl_conf_max_tls_version(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                                    mbedtls_ssl_protocol_version tls_version)\0A{\0A    conf->MBEDTLS_PRIVATE(max_tls_version) = tls_version;\0A}\0A\0A#if !defined(MBEDTLS_DEPRECATED_REMOVED)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the minimum accepted SSL/TLS protocol version\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           By default, all supported versions are accepted.\0A *                 Future versions of the library may disable older\0A *                 protocol versions by default if they become deprecated.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The following versions are supported (if enabled at\0A *                 compile time):\0A *                 - (D)TLS 1.2: \5Cp major = #MBEDTLS_SSL_MAJOR_VERSION_3,\0A *                   \5Cp minor = #MBEDTLS_SSL_MINOR_VERSION_3\0A *                 - TLS 1.3: \5Cp major = #MBEDTLS_SSL_MAJOR_VERSION_3,\0A *                   \5Cp minor = #MBEDTLS_SSL_MINOR_VERSION_4\0A *\0A *                 Note that the numbers in the constant names are the\0A *                 TLS internal protocol numbers, and the minor versions\0A *                 differ by one from the human-readable versions!\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Input outside of the SSL_MAX_XXXXX_VERSION and\0A *                 SSL_MIN_XXXXX_VERSION range is ignored.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           After the handshake, you can call\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_get_version_number() to see what version was\0A *                 negotiated.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This function is deprecated and has been replaced by\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_min_tls_version().\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam major    Major version number (#MBEDTLS_SSL_MAJOR_VERSION_3)\0A * \5Cparam minor    Minor version number\0A *                 (#MBEDTLS_SSL_MINOR_VERSION_3 for (D)TLS 1.2,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_SSL_MINOR_VERSION_4 for TLS 1.3)\0A */\0Avoid MBEDTLS_DEPRECATED mbedtls_ssl_conf_min_version(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf, int major,\0A                                                     int minor);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_DEPRECATED_REMOVED */\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the minimum supported version sent from the client side\0A *                 and/or accepted at the server side.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           After the handshake, you can call\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_get_version_number() to see what version was\0A *                 negotiated.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf         SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam tls_version  TLS protocol version number (\5Cc mbedtls_ssl_protocol_version)\0A *                     (#MBEDTLS_SSL_VERSION_UNKNOWN is not valid)\0A */\0Astatic inline void mbedtls_ssl_conf_min_tls_version(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                                    mbedtls_ssl_protocol_version tls_version)\0A{\0A    conf->MBEDTLS_PRIVATE(min_tls_version) = tls_version;\0A}\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_ENCRYPT_THEN_MAC)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief           Enable or disable Encrypt-then-MAC\0A *                  (Default: MBEDTLS_SSL_ETM_ENABLED)\0A *\0A * \5Cnote            This should always be enabled, it is a security\0A *                  improvement, and should not cause any interoperability\0A *                  issue (used only if the peer supports it too).\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf      SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam etm       MBEDTLS_SSL_ETM_ENABLED or MBEDTLS_SSL_ETM_DISABLED\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_encrypt_then_mac(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf, char etm);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_ENCRYPT_THEN_MAC */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_EXTENDED_MASTER_SECRET)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief           Enable or disable Extended Master Secret negotiation.\0A *                  (Default: MBEDTLS_SSL_EXTENDED_MS_ENABLED)\0A *\0A * \5Cnote            This should always be enabled, it is a security fix to the\0A *                  protocol, and should not cause any interoperability issue\0A *                  (used only if the peer supports it too).\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf      SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam ems       MBEDTLS_SSL_EXTENDED_MS_ENABLED or MBEDTLS_SSL_EXTENDED_MS_DISABLED\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_extended_master_secret(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf, char ems);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_EXTENDED_MASTER_SECRET */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Whether to send a list of acceptable CAs in\0A *                 CertificateRequest messages.\0A *                 (Default: do send)\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam cert_req_ca_list   MBEDTLS_SSL_CERT_REQ_CA_LIST_ENABLED or\0A *                          MBEDTLS_SSL_CERT_REQ_CA_LIST_DISABLED\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_cert_req_ca_list(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                       char cert_req_ca_list);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_MAX_FRAGMENT_LENGTH)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set the maximum fragment length to emit and/or negotiate.\0A *                 (Typical: the smaller of #MBEDTLS_SSL_IN_CONTENT_LEN and\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_SSL_OUT_CONTENT_LEN, usually `2^14` bytes)\0A *                 (Server: set maximum fragment length to emit,\0A *                 usually negotiated by the client during handshake)\0A *                 (Client: set maximum fragment length to emit *and*\0A *                 negotiate with the server during handshake)\0A *                 (Default: #MBEDTLS_SSL_MAX_FRAG_LEN_NONE)\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           On the client side, the maximum fragment length extension\0A *                 *will not* be used, unless the maximum fragment length has\0A *                 been set via this function to a value different than\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_SSL_MAX_FRAG_LEN_NONE.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           With TLS, this currently only affects ApplicationData (sent\0A *                 with \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_read()), not handshake messages.\0A *                 With DTLS, this affects both ApplicationData and handshake.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Defragmentation of TLS handshake messages is supported\0A *                 with some limitations. See the documentation of\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_handshake() for details.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This sets the maximum length for a record's payload,\0A *                 excluding record overhead that will be added to it, see\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_get_record_expansion().\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           For DTLS, it is also possible to set a limit for the total\0A *                 size of datagrams passed to the transport layer, including\0A *                 record overhead, see \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_set_mtu().\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam mfl_code Code for maximum fragment length (allowed values:\0A *                 MBEDTLS_SSL_MAX_FRAG_LEN_512,  MBEDTLS_SSL_MAX_FRAG_LEN_1024,\0A *                 MBEDTLS_SSL_MAX_FRAG_LEN_2048, MBEDTLS_SSL_MAX_FRAG_LEN_4096)\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         0 if successful or MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_BAD_INPUT_DATA\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_conf_max_frag_len(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf, unsigned char mfl_code);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_MAX_FRAGMENT_LENGTH */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Pick the ciphersuites order according to the second parameter\0A *                 in the SSL Server module (MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C).\0A *                 (Default, if never called: MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_CIPHERSUITE_ORDER_SERVER)\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam order    Server or client (MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_CIPHERSUITE_ORDER_SERVER\0A *                                or MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_CIPHERSUITE_ORDER_CLIENT)\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_preference_order(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf, int order);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_SESSION_TICKETS) && defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_CLI_C)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Enable / Disable TLS 1.2 session tickets (client only,\0A *                 TLS 1.2 only). Enabled by default.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           On server, use \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_conf_session_tickets_cb().\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam use_tickets   Enable or disable (#MBEDTLS_SSL_SESSION_TICKETS_ENABLED or\0A *                                         #MBEDTLS_SSL_SESSION_TICKETS_DISABLED)\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_session_tickets(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf, int use_tickets);\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_PROTO_TLS1_3)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief Enable / Disable handling of TLS 1.3 NewSessionTicket messages\0A *        (client only, TLS 1.3 only).\0A *\0A *        The handling of TLS 1.3 NewSessionTicket messages is disabled by\0A *        default.\0A *\0A *        In TLS 1.3, servers may send a NewSessionTicket message at any time,\0A *        and may send multiple NewSessionTicket messages. By default, TLS 1.3\0A *        clients ignore NewSessionTicket messages.\0A *\0A *        To support session tickets in TLS 1.3 clients, call this function\0A *        with #MBEDTLS_SSL_TLS1_3_SIGNAL_NEW_SESSION_TICKETS_ENABLED. When\0A *        this is enabled, when a client receives a NewSessionTicket message,\0A *        the next call to a message processing functions (notably\0A *        mbedtls_ssl_handshake() and mbedtls_ssl_read()) will return\0A *        #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_NEW_SESSION_TICKET. The client should then\0A *        call mbedtls_ssl_get_session() to retrieve the session ticket before\0A *        calling the same message processing function again.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf  SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam signal_new_session_tickets Enable or disable\0A *                                   (#MBEDTLS_SSL_TLS1_3_SIGNAL_NEW_SESSION_TICKETS_ENABLED or\0A *                                    #MBEDTLS_SSL_TLS1_3_SIGNAL_NEW_SESSION_TICKETS_DISABLED)\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_tls13_enable_signal_new_session_tickets(\0A    mbedtls_ssl_config *conf, int signal_new_session_tickets);\0A\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_PROTO_TLS1_3 */\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_SESSION_TICKETS && MBEDTLS_SSL_CLI_C */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_SESSION_TICKETS) && \5C\0A    defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C) && \5C\0A    defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_PROTO_TLS1_3)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief   Number of NewSessionTicket messages for the server to send\0A *          after handshake completion.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote    The default value is\0A *          \5Cc MBEDTLS_SSL_TLS1_3_DEFAULT_NEW_SESSION_TICKETS.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote    In case of a session resumption, this setting only partially apply.\0A *          At most one ticket is sent in that case to just renew the pool of\0A *          tickets of the client. The rationale is to avoid the number of\0A *          tickets on the server to become rapidly out of control when the\0A *          server has the same configuration for all its connection instances.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf    SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam num_tickets    Number of NewSessionTicket.\0A *\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_new_session_tickets(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                          uint16_t num_tickets);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_SESSION_TICKETS &&\0A          MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C &&\0A          MBEDTLS_SSL_PROTO_TLS1_3*/\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_RENEGOTIATION)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Enable / Disable renegotiation support for connection when\0A *                 initiated by peer\0A *                 (Default: MBEDTLS_SSL_RENEGOTIATION_DISABLED)\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        It is recommended to always disable renegotiation unless you\0A *                 know you need it and you know what you're doing. In the\0A *                 past, there have been several issues associated with\0A *                 renegotiation or a poor understanding of its properties.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Server-side, enabling renegotiation also makes the server\0A *                 susceptible to a resource DoS by a malicious client.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf    SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam renegotiation     Enable or disable (MBEDTLS_SSL_RENEGOTIATION_ENABLED or\0A *                                             MBEDTLS_SSL_RENEGOTIATION_DISABLED)\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_renegotiation(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf, int renegotiation);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_RENEGOTIATION */\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Prevent or allow legacy renegotiation.\0A *                 (Default: MBEDTLS_SSL_LEGACY_NO_RENEGOTIATION)\0A *\0A *                 MBEDTLS_SSL_LEGACY_NO_RENEGOTIATION allows connections to\0A *                 be established even if the peer does not support\0A *                 secure renegotiation, but does not allow renegotiation\0A *                 to take place if not secure.\0A *                 (Interoperable and secure option)\0A *\0A *                 MBEDTLS_SSL_LEGACY_ALLOW_RENEGOTIATION allows renegotiations\0A *                 with non-upgraded peers. Allowing legacy renegotiation\0A *                 makes the connection vulnerable to specific man in the\0A *                 middle attacks. (See RFC 5746)\0A *                 (Most interoperable and least secure option)\0A *\0A *                 MBEDTLS_SSL_LEGACY_BREAK_HANDSHAKE breaks off connections\0A *                 if peer does not support secure renegotiation. Results\0A *                 in interoperability issues with non-upgraded peers\0A *                 that do not support renegotiation altogether.\0A *                 (Most secure option, interoperability issues)\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam allow_legacy  Prevent or allow (SSL_NO_LEGACY_RENEGOTIATION,\0A *                                        SSL_ALLOW_LEGACY_RENEGOTIATION or\0A *                                        MBEDTLS_SSL_LEGACY_BREAK_HANDSHAKE)\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_legacy_renegotiation(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf, int allow_legacy);\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_RENEGOTIATION)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Enforce renegotiation requests.\0A *                 (Default: enforced, max_records = 16)\0A *\0A *                 When we request a renegotiation, the peer can comply or\0A *                 ignore the request. This function allows us to decide\0A *                 whether to enforce our renegotiation requests by closing\0A *                 the connection if the peer doesn't comply.\0A *\0A *                 However, records could already be in transit from the peer\0A *                 when the request is emitted. In order to increase\0A *                 reliability, we can accept a number of records before the\0A *                 expected handshake records.\0A *\0A *                 The optimal value is highly dependent on the specific usage\0A *                 scenario.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           With DTLS and server-initiated renegotiation, the\0A *                 HelloRequest is retransmitted every time mbedtls_ssl_read() times\0A *                 out or receives Application Data, until:\0A *                 - max_records records have beens seen, if it is >= 0, or\0A *                 - the number of retransmits that would happen during an\0A *                 actual handshake has been reached.\0A *                 Please remember the request might be lost a few times\0A *                 if you consider setting max_records to a really low value.\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        On client, the grace period can only happen during\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_read(), as opposed to mbedtls_ssl_write() and mbedtls_ssl_renegotiate()\0A *                 which always behave as if max_record was 0. The reason is,\0A *                 if we receive application data from the server, we need a\0A *                 place to write it, which only happens during mbedtls_ssl_read().\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam max_records Use MBEDTLS_SSL_RENEGOTIATION_NOT_ENFORCED if you don't want to\0A *                 enforce renegotiation, or a non-negative value to enforce\0A *                 it but allow for a grace period of max_records records.\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_renegotiation_enforced(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf, int max_records);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set record counter threshold for periodic renegotiation.\0A *                 (Default: 2^48 - 1)\0A *\0A *                 Renegotiation is automatically triggered when a record\0A *                 counter (outgoing or incoming) crosses the defined\0A *                 threshold. The default value is meant to prevent the\0A *                 connection from being closed when the counter is about to\0A *                 reached its maximal value (it is not allowed to wrap).\0A *\0A *                 Lower values can be used to enforce policies such as \22keys\0A *                 must be refreshed every N packets with cipher X\22.\0A *\0A *                 The renegotiation period can be disabled by setting\0A *                 conf->disable_renegotiation to\0A *                 MBEDTLS_SSL_RENEGOTIATION_DISABLED.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           When the configured transport is\0A *                 MBEDTLS_SSL_TRANSPORT_DATAGRAM the maximum renegotiation\0A *                 period is 2^48 - 1, and for MBEDTLS_SSL_TRANSPORT_STREAM,\0A *                 the maximum renegotiation period is 2^64 - 1.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam period   The threshold value: a big-endian 64-bit number.\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_renegotiation_period(mbedtls_ssl_config *conf,\0A                                           const unsigned char period[8]);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_RENEGOTIATION */\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Check if there is data already read from the\0A *                 underlying transport but not yet processed.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         0 if nothing's pending, 1 otherwise.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This is different in purpose and behaviour from\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_get_bytes_avail in that it considers\0A *                 any kind of unprocessed data, not only unread\0A *                 application data. If \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_get_bytes\0A *                 returns a non-zero value, this function will\0A *                 also signal pending data, but the converse does\0A *                 not hold. For example, in DTLS there might be\0A *                 further records waiting to be processed from\0A *                 the current underlying transport's datagram.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           If this function returns 1 (data pending), this\0A *                 does not imply that a subsequent call to\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_read will provide any data;\0A *                 e.g., the unprocessed data might turn out\0A *                 to be an alert or a handshake message.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This function is useful in the following situation:\0A *                 If the SSL/TLS module successfully returns from an\0A *                 operation - e.g. a handshake or an application record\0A *                 read - and you're awaiting incoming data next, you\0A *                 must not immediately idle on the underlying transport\0A *                 to have data ready, but you need to check the value\0A *                 of this function first. The reason is that the desired\0A *                 data might already be read but not yet processed.\0A *                 If, in contrast, a previous call to the SSL/TLS module\0A *                 returned MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ, it is not necessary\0A *                 to call this function, as the latter error code entails\0A *                 that all internal data has been processed.\0A *\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_check_pending(const mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Return the number of application data bytes\0A *                 remaining to be read from the current record.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         How many bytes are available in the application\0A *                 data record read buffer.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           When working over a datagram transport, this is\0A *                 useful to detect the current datagram's boundary\0A *                 in case \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_read has written the maximal\0A *                 amount of data fitting into the input buffer.\0A *\0A */\0Asize_t mbedtls_ssl_get_bytes_avail(const mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Return the result of the certificate verification\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      The SSL context to use.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc 0 if the certificate verification was successful.\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc -1u if the result is not available. This may happen\0A *                 e.g. if the handshake aborts early, or a verification\0A *                 callback returned a fatal error.\0A * \5Creturn         A bitwise combination of \5Cc MBEDTLS_X509_BADCERT_XXX\0A *                 and \5Cc MBEDTLS_X509_BADCRL_XXX failure flags; see x509.h.\0A */\0Auint32_t mbedtls_ssl_get_verify_result(const mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Return the id of the current ciphersuite\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         a ciphersuite id\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_get_ciphersuite_id_from_ssl(const mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Return the name of the current ciphersuite\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         a string containing the ciphersuite name\0A */\0Aconst char *mbedtls_ssl_get_ciphersuite(const mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Return the (D)TLS protocol version negotiated in the\0A *                 given connection.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           If you call this function too early during the initial\0A *                 handshake, before the two sides have agreed on a version,\0A *                 this function returns #MBEDTLS_SSL_VERSION_UNKNOWN.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      The SSL context to query.\0A * \5Creturn         The negotiated protocol version.\0A */\0Astatic inline mbedtls_ssl_protocol_version mbedtls_ssl_get_version_number(\0A    const mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl)\0A{\0A    return ssl->MBEDTLS_PRIVATE(tls_version);\0A}\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Return the current TLS version\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         a string containing the TLS version\0A */\0Aconst char *mbedtls_ssl_get_version(const mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Return the (maximum) number of bytes added by the record\0A *                 layer: header + encryption/MAC overhead (inc. padding)\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         Current maximum record expansion in bytes\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_get_record_expansion(const mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Return the current maximum outgoing record payload in bytes.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The logic to determine the maximum outgoing record payload is\0A *                 version-specific. It takes into account various factors, such as\0A *                 the mbedtls_config.h setting \5Cc MBEDTLS_SSL_OUT_CONTENT_LEN, extensions\0A *                 such as the max fragment length or record size limit extension if\0A *                 used, and for DTLS the path MTU as configured and current\0A *                 record expansion.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           With DTLS, \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_write() will return an error if\0A *                 called with a larger length value.\0A *                 With TLS, \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_write() will fragment the input if\0A *                 necessary and return the number of bytes written; it is up\0A *                 to the caller to call \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_write() again in\0A *                 order to send the remaining bytes if any.\0A *\0A * \5Csa             mbedtls_ssl_get_max_out_record_payload()\0A * \5Csa             mbedtls_ssl_get_record_expansion()\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         Current maximum payload for an outgoing record,\0A *                 or a negative error code.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_get_max_out_record_payload(const mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Return the current maximum incoming record payload in bytes.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The logic to determine the maximum incoming record payload is\0A *                 version-specific. It takes into account various factors, such as\0A *                 the mbedtls_config.h setting \5Cc MBEDTLS_SSL_IN_CONTENT_LEN, extensions\0A *                 such as the max fragment length extension or record size limit\0A *                 extension if used, and the current record expansion.\0A *\0A * \5Csa             mbedtls_ssl_set_mtu()\0A * \5Csa             mbedtls_ssl_get_max_in_record_payload()\0A * \5Csa             mbedtls_ssl_get_record_expansion()\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         Current maximum payload for an incoming record,\0A *                 or a negative error code.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_get_max_in_record_payload(const mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_X509_CRT_PARSE_C)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Return the peer certificate from the current connection.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam  ssl     The SSL context to use. This must be initialized and setup.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         The current peer certificate, if available.\0A *                 The returned certificate is owned by the SSL context and\0A *                 is valid only until the next call to the SSL API.\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc NULL if no peer certificate is available. This might\0A *                 be because the chosen ciphersuite doesn't use CRTs\0A *                 (PSK-based ciphersuites, for example), or because\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_SSL_KEEP_PEER_CERTIFICATE has been disabled,\0A *                 allowing the stack to free the peer's CRT to save memory.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           For one-time inspection of the peer's certificate during\0A *                 the handshake, consider registering an X.509 CRT verification\0A *                 callback through mbedtls_ssl_conf_verify() instead of calling\0A *                 this function. Using mbedtls_ssl_conf_verify() also comes at\0A *                 the benefit of allowing you to influence the verification\0A *                 process, for example by masking expected and tolerated\0A *                 verification failures.\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        You must not use the pointer returned by this function\0A *                 after any further call to the SSL API, including\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_read() and mbedtls_ssl_write(); this is\0A *                 because the pointer might change during renegotiation,\0A *                 which happens transparently to the user.\0A *                 If you want to use the certificate across API calls,\0A *                 you must make a copy.\0A */\0Aconst mbedtls_x509_crt *mbedtls_ssl_get_peer_cert(const mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_X509_CRT_PARSE_C */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_CLI_C)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Export a session in order to resume it later.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      The SSL context representing the connection for which to\0A *                 to export a session structure for later resumption.\0A * \5Cparam session  The target structure in which to store the exported session.\0A *                 This must have been initialized with mbedtls_ssl_session_init()\0A *                 but otherwise be unused.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This function can handle a variety of mechanisms for session\0A *                 resumption: For TLS 1.2, both session ID-based resumption and\0A *                 ticket-based resumption will be considered. For TLS 1.3,\0A *                 sessions equate to tickets, and if session tickets are\0A *                 enabled (see #MBEDTLS_SSL_SESSION_TICKETS configuration\0A *                 option), this function exports the last received ticket and\0A *                 the exported session may be used to resume the TLS 1.3\0A *                 session. If session tickets are disabled, exported sessions\0A *                 cannot be used to resume a TLS 1.3 session.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc 0 if successful. In this case, \5Cp session can be used for\0A *                 session resumption by passing it to mbedtls_ssl_set_session(),\0A *                 and serialized for storage via mbedtls_ssl_session_save().\0A * \5Creturn         Another negative error code on other kinds of failure.\0A *\0A * \5Csa             mbedtls_ssl_set_session()\0A * \5Csa             mbedtls_ssl_session_save()\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_get_session(const mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                            mbedtls_ssl_session *session);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_CLI_C */\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Perform the SSL handshake\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc 0 if successful.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ or #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_WRITE\0A *                 if the handshake is incomplete and waiting for data to\0A *                 be available for reading from or writing to the underlying\0A *                 transport - in this case you must call this function again\0A *                 when the underlying transport is ready for the operation.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_ASYNC_IN_PROGRESS if an asynchronous\0A *                 operation is in progress (see\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_conf_async_private_cb()) - in this case you\0A *                 must call this function again when the operation is ready.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CRYPTO_IN_PROGRESS if a cryptographic\0A *                 operation is in progress (see mbedtls_ecp_set_max_ops()) -\0A *                 in this case you must call this function again to complete\0A *                 the handshake when you're done attending other tasks.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_HELLO_VERIFY_REQUIRED if DTLS is in use\0A *                 and the client did not demonstrate reachability yet - in\0A *                 this case you must stop using the context (see below).\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_NEW_SESSION_TICKET if a TLS 1.3\0A *                 NewSessionTicket message has been received. See the\0A *                 documentation of mbedtls_ssl_read() for more information\0A *                 about this error code.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_EARLY_DATA if early data, as\0A *                 defined in RFC 8446 (TLS 1.3 specification), has been\0A *                 received as part of the handshake. This is server specific\0A *                 and may occur only if the early data feature has been\0A *                 enabled on server (see mbedtls_ssl_conf_early_data()\0A *                 documentation). You must call mbedtls_ssl_read_early_data()\0A *                 to read the early data before resuming the handshake.\0A * \5Creturn         Another SSL error code - in this case you must stop using\0A *                 the context (see below).\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        If this function returns something other than\0A *                 \5Cc 0,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_WRITE,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_ASYNC_IN_PROGRESS or\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CRYPTO_IN_PROGRESS or\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_NEW_SESSION_TICKET or\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_EARLY_DATA,\0A *                 you must stop using the SSL context for reading or writing,\0A *                 and either free it or call \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_session_reset()\0A *                 on it before re-using it for a new connection; the current\0A *                 connection must be closed.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           If DTLS is in use, then you may choose to handle\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_HELLO_VERIFY_REQUIRED specially for logging\0A *                 purposes, as it is an expected return value rather than an\0A *                 actual error, but you still need to reset/free the context.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Remarks regarding event-driven DTLS:\0A *                 If the function returns #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ, no datagram\0A *                 from the underlying transport layer is currently being processed,\0A *                 and it is safe to idle until the timer or the underlying transport\0A *                 signal a new event. This is not true for a successful handshake,\0A *                 in which case the datagram of the underlying transport that is\0A *                 currently being processed might or might not contain further\0A *                 DTLS records.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           If #MBEDTLS_USE_PSA_CRYPTO is enabled, the PSA crypto\0A *                 subsystem must have been initialized by calling\0A *                 psa_crypto_init() before calling this function.\0A *                 Otherwise, the handshake may call psa_crypto_init()\0A *                 if a negotiation involving TLS 1.3 takes place (this may\0A *                 be the case even if TLS 1.3 is offered but eventually\0A *                 not selected).\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           In TLS, reception of fragmented handshake messages is\0A *                 supported with some limitations (those limitations do\0A *                 not apply to DTLS, where defragmentation is fully\0A *                 supported):\0A *                 - On an Mbed TLS server that only accepts TLS 1.2,\0A *                   the initial ClientHello message must not be fragmented.\0A *                   A TLS 1.2 ClientHello may be fragmented if the server\0A *                   also accepts TLS 1.3 connections (meaning\0A *                   that #MBEDTLS_SSL_PROTO_TLS1_3 enabled, and the\0A *                   accepted versions have not been restricted with\0A *                   mbedtls_ssl_conf_max_tls_version() or the like).\0A *                 - The first fragment of a handshake message must be\0A *                   at least 4 bytes long.\0A *                 - Non-handshake records must not be interleaved between\0A *                   the fragments of a handshake message. (This is permitted\0A *                   in TLS 1.2 but not in TLS 1.3, but Mbed TLS rejects it\0A *                   even in TLS 1.2.)\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_handshake(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          After calling mbedtls_ssl_handshake() to start the SSL\0A *                 handshake you can call this function to check whether the\0A *                 handshake is over for a given SSL context. This function\0A *                 should be also used to determine when to stop calling\0A *                 mbedtls_handshake_step() for that context.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc 1 if handshake is over, \5Cc 0 if it is still ongoing.\0A */\0Astatic inline int mbedtls_ssl_is_handshake_over(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl)\0A{\0A    return ssl->MBEDTLS_PRIVATE(state) >= MBEDTLS_SSL_HANDSHAKE_OVER;\0A}\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Perform a single step of the SSL handshake\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The state of the context (ssl->state) will be at\0A *                 the next state after this function returns \5Cc 0. Do not\0A *                 call this function if mbedtls_ssl_is_handshake_over()\0A *                 returns \5Cc 1.\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        Whilst in the past you may have used direct access to the\0A *                 context state (ssl->state) in order to ascertain when to\0A *                 stop calling this function and although you can still do\0A *                 so with something like ssl->MBEDTLS_PRIVATE(state) or by\0A *                 defining MBEDTLS_ALLOW_PRIVATE_ACCESS, this is now\0A *                 considered deprecated and could be broken in any future\0A *                 release. If you still find you have good reason for such\0A *                 direct access, then please do contact the team to explain\0A *                 this (raise an issue or post to the mailing list), so that\0A *                 we can add a solution to your problem that will be\0A *                 guaranteed to work in the future.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         See mbedtls_ssl_handshake().\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        If this function returns something other than \5Cc 0,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ, #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_WRITE,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_ASYNC_IN_PROGRESS,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CRYPTO_IN_PROGRESS or\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_NEW_SESSION_TICKET or\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_EARLY_DATA, you must stop using\0A *                 the SSL context for reading or writing, and either free it\0A *                 or call \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_session_reset() on it before\0A *                 re-using it for a new connection; the current connection\0A *                 must be closed.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_handshake_step(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_RENEGOTIATION)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Initiate an SSL renegotiation on the running connection.\0A *                 Client: perform the renegotiation right now.\0A *                 Server: request renegotiation, which will be performed\0A *                 during the next call to mbedtls_ssl_read() if honored by\0A *                 client.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         0 if successful, or any mbedtls_ssl_handshake() return\0A *                 value except #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CLIENT_RECONNECT that can't\0A *                 happen during a renegotiation.\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        If this function returns something other than \5Cc 0,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ, #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_WRITE,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_ASYNC_IN_PROGRESS or\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CRYPTO_IN_PROGRESS, you must stop using\0A *                 the SSL context for reading or writing, and either free it\0A *                 or call \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_session_reset() on it before\0A *                 re-using it for a new connection; the current connection\0A *                 must be closed.\0A *\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_renegotiate(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_RENEGOTIATION */\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Read at most 'len' application data bytes\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A * \5Cparam buf      buffer that will hold the data\0A * \5Cparam len      maximum number of bytes to read\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         The (positive) number of bytes read if successful.\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc 0 if the read end of the underlying transport was closed\0A *                 without sending a CloseNotify beforehand, which might happen\0A *                 because of various reasons (internal error of an underlying\0A *                 stack, non-conformant peer not sending a CloseNotify and\0A *                 such) - in this case you must stop using the context\0A *                 (see below).\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_PEER_CLOSE_NOTIFY if the underlying\0A *                 transport is still functional, but the peer has\0A *                 acknowledged to not send anything anymore.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ or #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_WRITE\0A *                 if the handshake is incomplete and waiting for data to\0A *                 be available for reading from or writing to the underlying\0A *                 transport - in this case you must call this function again\0A *                 when the underlying transport is ready for the operation.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_ASYNC_IN_PROGRESS if an asynchronous\0A *                 operation is in progress (see\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_conf_async_private_cb()) - in this case you\0A *                 must call this function again when the operation is ready.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CRYPTO_IN_PROGRESS if a cryptographic\0A *                 operation is in progress (see mbedtls_ecp_set_max_ops()) -\0A *                 in this case you must call this function again to complete\0A *                 the handshake when you're done attending other tasks.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CLIENT_RECONNECT if we're at the server\0A *                 side of a DTLS connection and the client is initiating a\0A *                 new connection using the same source port. See below.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_NEW_SESSION_TICKET if a TLS 1.3\0A *                 NewSessionTicket message has been received.\0A *                 This error code is only returned on the client side. It is\0A *                 only returned if handling of TLS 1.3 NewSessionTicket\0A *                 messages has been enabled through\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_conf_tls13_enable_signal_new_session_tickets().\0A *                 This error code indicates that a TLS 1.3 NewSessionTicket\0A *                 message has been received and parsed successfully by the\0A *                 client. The ticket data can be retrieved from the SSL\0A *                 context by calling mbedtls_ssl_get_session(). It remains\0A *                 available until the next call to mbedtls_ssl_read().\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_EARLY_DATA if early data, as\0A *                 defined in RFC 8446 (TLS 1.3 specification), has been\0A *                 received as part of the handshake. This is server specific\0A *                 and may occur only if the early data feature has been\0A *                 enabled on server (see mbedtls_ssl_conf_early_data()\0A *                 documentation). You must call mbedtls_ssl_read_early_data()\0A *                 to read the early data before resuming the handshake.\0A * \5Creturn         Another SSL error code - in this case you must stop using\0A *                 the context (see below).\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        If this function returns something other than\0A *                 a positive value,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_WRITE,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_ASYNC_IN_PROGRESS,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CRYPTO_IN_PROGRESS,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CLIENT_RECONNECT or\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_NEW_SESSION_TICKET or\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_EARLY_DATA,\0A *                 you must stop using the SSL context for reading or writing,\0A *                 and either free it or call \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_session_reset()\0A *                 on it before re-using it for a new connection; the current\0A *                 connection must be closed.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           When this function returns #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CLIENT_RECONNECT\0A *                 (which can only happen server-side), it means that a client\0A *                 is initiating a new connection using the same source port.\0A *                 You can either treat that as a connection close and wait\0A *                 for the client to resend a ClientHello, or directly\0A *                 continue with \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_handshake() with the same\0A *                 context (as it has been reset internally). Either way, you\0A *                 must make sure this is seen by the application as a new\0A *                 connection: application state, if any, should be reset, and\0A *                 most importantly the identity of the client must be checked\0A *                 again. WARNING: not validating the identity of the client\0A *                 again, or not transmitting the new identity to the\0A *                 application layer, would allow authentication bypass!\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Remarks regarding event-driven DTLS:\0A *                 - If the function returns #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ, no datagram\0A *                   from the underlying transport layer is currently being processed,\0A *                   and it is safe to idle until the timer or the underlying transport\0A *                   signal a new event.\0A *                 - This function may return MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ even if data was\0A *                   initially available on the underlying transport, as this data may have\0A *                   been only e.g. duplicated messages or a renegotiation request.\0A *                   Therefore, you must be prepared to receive MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ even\0A *                   when reacting to an incoming-data event from the underlying transport.\0A *                 - On success, the datagram of the underlying transport that is currently\0A *                   being processed may contain further DTLS records. You should call\0A *                   \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_check_pending to check for remaining records.\0A *\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_read(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl, unsigned char *buf, size_t len);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Try to write exactly 'len' application data bytes\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        This function will do partial writes in some cases. If the\0A *                 return value is non-negative but less than length, the\0A *                 function must be called again with updated arguments:\0A *                 buf + ret, len - ret (if ret is the return value) until\0A *                 it returns a value equal to the last 'len' argument.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A * \5Cparam buf      buffer holding the data\0A * \5Cparam len      how many bytes must be written\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         The (non-negative) number of bytes actually written if\0A *                 successful (may be less than \5Cp len).\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ or #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_WRITE\0A *                 if the handshake is incomplete and waiting for data to\0A *                 be available for reading from or writing to the underlying\0A *                 transport - in this case you must call this function again\0A *                 when the underlying transport is ready for the operation.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_ASYNC_IN_PROGRESS if an asynchronous\0A *                 operation is in progress (see\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_conf_async_private_cb()) - in this case you\0A *                 must call this function again when the operation is ready.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CRYPTO_IN_PROGRESS if a cryptographic\0A *                 operation is in progress (see mbedtls_ecp_set_max_ops()) -\0A *                 in this case you must call this function again to complete\0A *                 the handshake when you're done attending other tasks.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_NEW_SESSION_TICKET if a TLS 1.3\0A *                 NewSessionTicket message has been received. See the\0A *                 documentation of mbedtls_ssl_read() for more information\0A *                 about this error code.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_EARLY_DATA if early data, as\0A *                 defined in RFC 8446 (TLS 1.3 specification), has been\0A *                 received as part of the handshake. This is server specific\0A *                 and may occur only if the early data feature has been\0A *                 enabled on server (see mbedtls_ssl_conf_early_data()\0A *                 documentation). You must call mbedtls_ssl_read_early_data()\0A *                 to read the early data before resuming the handshake.\0A * \5Creturn         Another SSL error code - in this case you must stop using\0A *                 the context (see below).\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        If this function returns something other than\0A *                 a non-negative value,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_WRITE,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_ASYNC_IN_PROGRESS,\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CRYPTO_IN_PROGRESS or\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_NEW_SESSION_TICKET or\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_EARLY_DATA,\0A *                 you must stop using the SSL context for reading or writing,\0A *                 and either free it or call \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_session_reset()\0A *                 on it before re-using it for a new connection; the current\0A *                 connection must be closed.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           When this function returns #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_WRITE/READ,\0A *                 it must be called later with the *same* arguments,\0A *                 until it returns a value greater than or equal to 0. When\0A *                 the function returns #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_WRITE there may be\0A *                 some partial data in the output buffer, however this is not\0A *                 yet sent.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           If the requested length is greater than the maximum\0A *                 fragment length (either the built-in limit or the one set\0A *                 or negotiated with the peer), then:\0A *                 - with TLS, less bytes than requested are written.\0A *                 - with DTLS, MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_BAD_INPUT_DATA is returned.\0A *                 \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_get_max_out_record_payload() may be used to\0A *                 query the active maximum fragment length.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Attempting to write 0 bytes will result in an empty TLS\0A *                 application record being sent.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_write(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl, const unsigned char *buf, size_t len);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief           Send an alert message\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl       SSL context\0A * \5Cparam level     The alert level of the message\0A *                  (MBEDTLS_SSL_ALERT_LEVEL_WARNING or MBEDTLS_SSL_ALERT_LEVEL_FATAL)\0A * \5Cparam message   The alert message (SSL_ALERT_MSG_*)\0A *\0A * \5Creturn          0 if successful, or a specific SSL error code.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           If this function returns something other than 0 or\0A *                 MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ/WRITE, you must stop using\0A *                 the SSL context for reading or writing, and either free it or\0A *                 call \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_session_reset() on it before re-using it\0A *                 for a new connection; the current connection must be closed.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_send_alert_message(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                                   unsigned char level,\0A                                   unsigned char message);\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Notify the peer that the connection is being closed\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A *\0A * \5Creturn          0 if successful, or a specific SSL error code.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           If this function returns something other than 0 or\0A *                 MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ/WRITE, you must stop using\0A *                 the SSL context for reading or writing, and either free it or\0A *                 call \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_session_reset() on it before re-using it\0A *                 for a new connection; the current connection must be closed.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_close_notify(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_EARLY_DATA)\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Read at most 'len' bytes of early data\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This API is server specific.\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        Early data is defined in the TLS 1.3 specification, RFC 8446.\0A *                 IMPORTANT NOTE from section 2.3 of the specification:\0A *\0A *                 The security properties for 0-RTT data are weaker than\0A *                 those for other kinds of TLS data. Specifically:\0A *                 - This data is not forward secret, as it is encrypted\0A *                   solely under keys derived using the offered PSK.\0A *                 - There are no guarantees of non-replay between connections.\0A *                   Protection against replay for ordinary TLS 1.3 1-RTT data\0A *                   is provided via the server's Random value, but 0-RTT data\0A *                   does not depend on the ServerHello and therefore has\0A *                   weaker guarantees. This is especially relevant if the\0A *                   data is authenticated either with TLS client\0A *                   authentication or inside the application protocol. The\0A *                   same warnings apply to any use of the\0A *                   early_exporter_master_secret.\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        Mbed TLS does not implement any of the anti-replay defenses\0A *                 defined in section 8 of the TLS 1.3 specification:\0A *                 single-use of tickets or ClientHello recording within a\0A *                 given time window.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This function is used in conjunction with\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_handshake(), mbedtls_ssl_handshake_step(),\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_read() and mbedtls_ssl_write() to read early\0A *                 data when these functions return\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_EARLY_DATA.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context, it must have been initialized and set up.\0A * \5Cparam buf      buffer that will hold the data\0A * \5Cparam len      maximum number of bytes to read\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         The (positive) number of bytes read if successful.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_BAD_INPUT_DATA if input data is invalid.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CANNOT_READ_EARLY_DATA if it is not\0A *                 possible to read early data for the SSL context \5Cp ssl. Note\0A *                 that this function is intended to be called for an SSL\0A *                 context \5Cp ssl only after a call to mbedtls_ssl_handshake(),\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_handshake_step(), mbedtls_ssl_read() or\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_write() for \5Cp ssl that has returned\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_RECEIVED_EARLY_DATA.\0A */\0Aint mbedtls_ssl_read_early_data(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                                unsigned char *buf, size_t len);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_CLI_C)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Try to write exactly 'len' application data bytes while\0A *                 performing the handshake (early data).\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning        Early data is defined in the TLS 1.3 specification, RFC 8446.\0A *                 IMPORTANT NOTE from section 2.3 of the specification:\0A *\0A *                 The security properties for 0-RTT data are weaker than\0A *                 those for other kinds of TLS data. Specifically:\0A *                 - This data is not forward secret, as it is encrypted\0A *                   solely under keys derived using the offered PSK.\0A *                 - There are no guarantees of non-replay between connections.\0A *                   Protection against replay for ordinary TLS 1.3 1-RTT data\0A *                   is provided via the server's Random value, but 0-RTT data\0A *                   does not depend on the ServerHello and therefore has\0A *                   weaker guarantees. This is especially relevant if the\0A *                   data is authenticated either with TLS client\0A *                   authentication or inside the application protocol. The\0A *                   same warnings apply to any use of the\0A *                   early_exporter_master_secret.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This function behaves mainly as mbedtls_ssl_write(). The\0A *                 specification of mbedtls_ssl_write() relevant to TLS 1.3\0A *                 (thus not the parts specific to (D)TLS1.2) applies to this\0A *                 function and the present documentation is mainly restricted\0A *                 to the differences with mbedtls_ssl_write(). One noticeable\0A *                 difference though is that mbedtls_ssl_write() aims to\0A *                 complete the handshake before to write application data\0A *                 while mbedtls_ssl_write_early() aims to drive the handshake\0A *                 just past the point where it is not possible to send early\0A *                 data anymore.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A * \5Cparam buf      buffer holding the data\0A * \5Cparam len      how many bytes must be written\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         The (non-negative) number of bytes actually written if\0A *                 successful (may be less than \5Cp len).\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         One additional specific error code compared to\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_write():\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CANNOT_WRITE_EARLY_DATA.\0A *\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CANNOT_WRITE_EARLY_DATA is returned when it\0A *                 is not possible to write early data for the SSL context\0A *                 \5Cp ssl.\0A *\0A *                 It may have been possible and it is not possible\0A *                 anymore because the client received the server Finished\0A *                 message, the server rejected early data or the maximum\0A *                 number of allowed early data for the PSK in use has been\0A *                 reached.\0A *\0A *                 It may never have been possible and will never be possible\0A *                 for the SSL context \5Cp ssl because the use of early data\0A *                 is disabled for that context or more generally the context\0A *                 is not suitably configured to enable early data or the first\0A *                 call to the function was done while the handshake was\0A *                 already completed.\0A *\0A *                 It is not possible to write early data for the SSL context\0A *                 \5Cp ssl and any subsequent call to this API will return this\0A *                 error code. But this does not preclude for using it with\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_write(), mbedtls_ssl_read() or\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_handshake() and the handshake can be\0A *                 completed by calling one of these APIs.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This function may write early data only if the SSL context\0A *                 has been configured for the handshake with a PSK for which\0A *                 early data is allowed.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           To maximize the number of early data that can be written in\0A *                 the course of the handshake, it is expected that this\0A *                 function starts the handshake for the SSL context \5Cp ssl.\0A *                 But this is not mandatory.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This function does not provide any information